+/*
+==================
+VKQ_VR_CenterPrintText -- overlay 0024 (VR R6.1 item 2)
+
+The engine's live centerprint, handed to the shell so it can put it on a
+head-locked panel inside the VR space.
+
+the user, on 1.0.7.9: "when you get a message from the game (like if you try to
+open a door, and it'll say this is opened elsewhere) - hear the message sound,
+but can't read it." He is right and the cause is charter A9 working as designed:
+VR world frames suppress the ENTIRE engine 2D composite (SCR_DrawGUI's
+vkq_vr_suppress_2d early return), and SCR_CheckDrawCenterString lives inside it.
+The sound is a server event and survives; the text was drawn into a layer nobody
+was showing.
+
+The predicate below is SCR_CheckDrawCenterString's, term for term, minus the
+intermission clause: an intermission in VR is not a world frame at all
+(SCR_VRPresentMode -> REASON_INTERMISSION), so the whole flat composite —
+finale text included — is already on the world-locked panel and drawing it a
+second time on a head-locked one would double it. Keeping the predicate a copy
+of the drawer's rather than a fresh opinion is deliberate: the two must agree
+about what "there is a message right now" means, or VR and 2D will disagree
+about whether the player was told something.
+==================
+*/
+int VKQ_VR_CenterPrintText (char *out, int len)
+{
+	if (!out || len <= 0)
+		return 0;
+	out[0] = 0;
+	if (scr_centertime_off <= cl.time)
+		return 0;
+	if (cl.intermission)
+		return 0;
+	if (key_dest != key_game)
+		return 0;
+	if (cl.paused && (!cls.demoplayback || cls.demospeed > 0.f))
+		return 0;
+	if (!scr_centerstring[0])
+		return 0;
+	q_strlcpy (out, scr_centerstring, len);
+	return 1;
 }
